answer:
@Module – class that provides dependencies.
@Provides – method inside @Module that returns the dependency instance.
@Inject – requests a dependency injection (used on constructor, field, or method).
@Component – bridge between @Module and @Inject.
@Singleton – ensures only one instance of the dependency is created.
@Scope – defines a custom lifecycle scope.
@Qualifier – allows multiple instances of the same type.
@Binds – binds an interface to its implementation in an abstract module.

@Test – marks a method as a test case.
@Before – runs before each test (setup).
@BeforeClass – runs once before all tests in the class.
@After – runs after each test (teardown).
@AfterClass – runs once after all tests.
@Ignore – skips the test.
@Test(timeout=500) – fails the test if it exceeds the specified time.

SupervisorScope is a coroutine builder that creates a new child scope and acts as a supervisor for coroutines launched within it.

Unlike a regular coroutine scope, if a child coroutine fails with an exception, the SupervisorScope does not cancel the remaining child coroutines. Each child is independent.

Example:
suspend fun myCoroutine() {
    supervisorScope {
        launch {
            // do some work
        }
        launch {
            // do some other work — runs even if the first launch fails
        }
    }
}

By default, all classes and functions in Kotlin are final, meaning they cannot be inherited or overridden. To allow inheritance or overriding, you must explicitly mark the class or function with the open keyword.

Example:
open class Animal {
    open fun speak() { }
}

class Dog : Animal() {
    override fun speak() { }
}

A higher-order function is a function that either takes another function as a parameter, returns a function, or both.

Example:
fun passMeFunction(abc: () -> Unit) {
    // executes the passed function
    abc()
}

Higher-order functions are the foundation for features like scope functions (let, apply, run), collection operations (map, filter), and coroutine builders (launch, async).

Primary Constructor:
Defined in the class header. It cannot contain any code directly; initialization logic goes in an init{} block.
Example: class Person(val name: String, val age: Int)

Secondary Constructor:
Defined inside the class body using the constructor keyword. You must explicitly call the primary constructor from a secondary constructor using this().

Example:
class Person(val name: String) {
    constructor(name: String, age: Int) : this(name) {
        // additional setup
    }
}

In Kotlin, a Singleton is created using the object keyword. The Kotlin compiler ensures only one instance is ever created.

Example:
object DatabaseManager {
    fun getConnection() { }
}

This is equivalent to Java's double-checked locking Singleton pattern but is much simpler and thread-safe by default.

Kotlin does not have a static keyword like Java. Instead, we use a companion object to define members that belong to the class itself rather than to any instance. A companion object allows you to call methods or access properties without creating an object of the class.

Example:
class Mentor {
    companion object {
        fun guide() {
            println("Guiding...")
        }
    }
}

// Call without creating an instance:
Mentor.guide()

This is the Kotlin equivalent of Java's static methods and static fields.

crossinline is used in inline functions to prevent non-local returns from a lambda parameter.

By default, a lambda passed to an inline function can use return to exit the outer calling function (non-local return). crossinline disallows this, ensuring the lambda can only return from itself.

Example:
inline fun execute(crossinline action: () -> Unit) {
    val runnable = Runnable { action() }
    runnable.run()
}

Use crossinline when the lambda will be executed in a different execution context, such as inside a Runnable or a callback, where a non-local return would be unsafe or ambiguous.

A Collector is the consumer of a Flow. It collects the items emitted by the Flow Builder after they have been transformed by operators.

Example:
viewModelScope.launch {
    myFlow
        .map { it * 2 }
        .collect { value ->
            println(value)
        }
}

The collect{} block is the most common terminal operator and acts as the collector. The flow does not start emitting until a collector begins collecting.

The flowOn operator controls which thread or dispatcher the upstream flow (the emission and transformation logic) runs on. It does not affect the collector, which runs on the scope's dispatcher.

Example:
val flow = flow {
    (0..10).forEach {
        delay(500)
        emit(it)
    }
}
.flowOn(Dispatchers.Default)

viewModelScope.launch {
    flow.collect { value ->
        // collected on Main thread (viewModelScope default)
        println(value)
    }
}

Here, the emission happens on Dispatchers.Default (background), but collection happens on the main thread.

The zip operator combines the emissions of two Flow collections together via a specified function and emits a single item for each combined pair.

Key advantage: Both flows run in parallel, and the result is delivered in a single callback only when both flows have emitted their corresponding values.

Example:
fun startLongRunningTask() {
    viewModelScope.launch {
        doLongRunningTaskOne()
            .zip(doLongRunningTaskTwo()) { resultOne, resultTwo ->
                return@zip resultOne + resultTwo
            }
            .flowOn(Dispatchers.Default)
            .catch { e ->
                // handle exception
            }
            .collect {
                // combined result
            }
    }
}

Use zip when you need to run two tasks in parallel and combine their results once both complete.

In Kotlin Flow, exceptions can be caught using the catch operator. It intercepts any exception thrown upstream (in the flow builder or operators) and lets you handle it gracefully without crashing.

Example:
(1..5).asFlow()
    .map {
        check(it != 3) { "Error at value $it" } // throws exception when value is 3
        it * it
    }
    .onCompletion {
        println("Flow completed")
    }
    .catch { e ->
        println("Caught exception: $e")
    }
    .collect {
        println(it)
    }

Note: The catch operator only catches exceptions from upstream. Exceptions thrown inside the collect block are not caught by catch and must be handled separately with a try-catch.

An init block is an initializer block that runs immediately after the primary constructor executes. Since you cannot write logic directly inside a primary constructor in Kotlin, the init block is the place to perform initialization operations.

A class can have one or more init blocks, and they execute in the order they appear in the class body.

Example:
class Person(val name: String) {
    init {
        println("Person created with name: $name")
    }
    init {
        println("Second init block runs after the first")
    }
}

val p = Person("Alice")
// Output:
// Person created with name: Alice
// Second init block runs after the first

@JvmStatic:
Tells the compiler to generate a true static method for a companion object function, making it accessible from Java code as a static method.
Example:
class Utils {
    companion object {
        @JvmStatic
        fun getAddress(): String = "MindOrks"
    }
}
// In Java: Utils.getAddress() — works as a static call

@JvmOverloads:
Generates overloaded versions of a Kotlin function that has default parameter values, so Java callers can call it without providing all arguments.
Example:
data class Event(val name: String, val date: Date = Date())
// Java can now call: new Event("MindOrks") without the date parameter

@JvmField:
Exposes a Kotlin property as a plain Java field, removing the auto-generated getter and setter. Used when Java code needs direct field access without calling get/set methods.

A SAM (Single Abstract Method) interface, also called a Functional Interface, is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method.

In Kotlin, it is declared using the fun interface keyword. SAM conversion allows you to pass a concise lambda expression instead of writing a verbose anonymous object.

Example:
fun interface OnClickAction {
    fun onClick()
}

// Without SAM (verbose anonymous object):
val action = object : OnClickAction {
    override fun onClick() {
        println("Clicked")
    }
}

// With SAM conversion (concise lambda):
val action = OnClickAction { println("Clicked") }

Kotlin also supports SAM conversion for Java interfaces like Runnable and OnClickListener, which is why you can write:
button.setOnClickListener { view -> println("Button clicked") }
instead of creating an anonymous OnClickListener object.

A Lambda in Kotlin is an anonymous function that can be stored in a variable, passed as a parameter, or returned from another function. It is mainly used to write concise, reusable code without creating separate function declarations.

Lambdas are commonly used with higher-order functions, collection operations like map and filter, and click listeners.

Lambda stored in a variable:
val square: (Int) -> Int = { value -> value * value }
println(square(3)) // 9

val addition: (Int, Int) -> Int = { num1, num2 -> num1 + num2 }
println(addition(4, 6)) // 10

Lambda passed as a parameter:
fun passFunction(action: () -> Unit) {
    action()
}
passFunction { println("Lambda executed") }

Lambda returned from a function:
fun returnAddFunction(): (Int, Int) -> Int {
    return { a, b -> a + b }
}
val add = returnAddFunction()
println(add(10, 20)) // 30

Real-world Android example using lambdas as callbacks in a ViewModel:
class RecipeViewModel : ViewModel() {
    fun fetchRecipes(
        onSuccess: (List<Recipe>) -> Unit,
        onError: (String) -> Unit
    ) {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            try {
                val recipes = repository.getRecipes()
                onSuccess(recipes)
            } catch (e: Exception) {
                onError(e.message ?: "Unknown Error")
            }
        }
    }
}

// Calling the function with lambda arguments:
viewModel.fetchRecipes(
    onSuccess = { recipes -> adapter.submitList(recipes) },
    onError = { message -> showToast(message) }
)

setContent is the Compose equivalent of setContentView in the traditional XML-based approach. It is called inside onCreate() of an Activity and allows you to display composable UI to the user.

Instead of inflating an XML layout, setContent accepts a composable function that defines the UI.

Example:
class MainActivity : ComponentActivity() {
    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        setContent {
            MyAppTheme {
                HomeScreen()
            }
        }
    }
}

remember is used to store a value in memory so it is not reset when the composable recomposes.

When state changes, Compose re-runs the composable function. Without remember, any variable declared inside the composable would be re-initialized to its default value on every recomposition.

With remember, the value is preserved across recompositions as long as the composable stays in the composition.

Example:
@Composable
fun Counter() {
    var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }
    Button(onClick = { count++ }) {
        Text("Count: $count")
    }
}

Note: remember does NOT survive configuration changes like screen rotation. Use rememberSaveable for that.

@Preview is an annotation used in Jetpack Compose to preview how a composable will look directly inside Android Studio without running the app on a device or emulator.

Key points:
- The annotated function must be a @Composable function with no required parameters (or use default values).
- You can have multiple @Preview annotations with different configurations (dark mode, font scale, device size).
- Previews are only for development purposes and are not included in the final APK.

Example:
@Preview(showBackground = true)
@Composable
fun GreetingPreview() {
    Greeting(name = "Abhijeet")
}

derivedStateOf is used to create a new state that is computed from one or more existing states. It is useful when the source state changes frequently but the derived result changes less often, preventing unnecessary recompositions.

Example:
Suppose scrollState updates on every pixel scrolled, but you only want to recompose when the user has scrolled another 100 pixels:

val counter = remember {
    derivedStateOf {
        (scrollState / 100).roundToInt()
    }
}

Text(counter.value.toString())

Without derivedStateOf, the Text would recompose on every single pixel of scroll. With derivedStateOf, it only recomposes when counter actually changes — once every 100 pixels. This significantly reduces unnecessary recompositions.

rememberCoroutineScope() provides a CoroutineScope that is tied to the composable's lifecycle and can be used to launch coroutines from non-composable contexts such as button click callbacks.

Why it is needed:
LaunchedEffect can only be used directly inside a composable body and cannot be triggered from a click handler. rememberCoroutineScope solves this by giving you a scope you can use anywhere inside the composable, including inside onClick.

The scope is remembered across recompositions and is automatically cancelled when the composable leaves the composition, preventing memory leaks.

Example:
@Composable
fun MyScreen() {
    val scope = rememberCoroutineScope()
    val snackbarHostState = remember { SnackbarHostState() }

    Button(onClick = {
        scope.launch {
            snackbarHostState.showSnackbar("Button clicked!")
        }
    }) {
        Text("Show Snackbar")
    }
}

State hoisting is a design pattern in Jetpack Compose where state is moved from a child composable up to its parent. The parent holds the state and passes down the current value and an update callback (lambda) to the child.

This makes the child composable stateless — it only receives data and reports events, making it reusable, easier to test, and easier to preview.

Example — without hoisting (stateful, not reusable):
@Composable
fun Counter() {
    var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }
    Button(onClick = { count++ }) { Text("Count: $count") }
}

Example — with hoisting (stateless, reusable):
@Composable
fun Counter(count: Int, onIncrement: () -> Unit) {
    Button(onClick = onIncrement) { Text("Count: $count") }
}

// Parent holds the state:
@Composable
fun CounterScreen() {
    var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }
    Counter(count = count, onIncrement = { count++ })
}

LaunchedEffect is a side-effect API used to safely run suspend functions (coroutines) inside a Composable.

Behavior:
- Runs the coroutine block when the composable enters the composition.
- Restarts the coroutine if any of its keys change.
- Cancels the running coroutine if the composable leaves the composition.

Example — fetch data when screen opens:
@Composable
fun UserScreen(userId: String, viewModel: UserViewModel) {
    LaunchedEffect(userId) {
        viewModel.loadUser(userId)
    }
}

Key point: LaunchedEffect can only be called from the composable body, not from event callbacks like onClick. Use rememberCoroutineScope for that.

DisposableEffect is a side-effect API in Jetpack Compose used to perform setup and cleanup operations tied to the composable's lifecycle.

Behavior:
- Runs the setup block when the composable enters the composition or when its key changes.
- The onDispose block runs when the composable leaves the composition or before the effect restarts due to a key change.

Commonly used for: registering and unregistering listeners, observers, or subscriptions.

Example — register and unregister a lifecycle observer:
@Composable
fun LifecycleObserverEffect(lifecycleOwner: LifecycleOwner) {
    DisposableEffect(lifecycleOwner) {
        val observer = LifecycleEventObserver { _, event ->
            println("Event: $event")
        }
        lifecycleOwner.lifecycle.addObserver(observer)
        onDispose {
            lifecycleOwner.lifecycle.removeObserver(observer)
        }
    }
}

mutableStateOf is an observable state holder built into Jetpack Compose. When its value changes, Compose automatically triggers recomposition for every composable function that reads that state, keeping the UI in sync with the underlying data.

Usage:
var name by remember { mutableStateOf("") }

Three ways to use it:
val state = remember { mutableStateOf(0) }
// Access: state.value

var state by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }
// Access: state directly (requires import for property delegate)

val (state, setState) = remember { mutableStateOf(0) }
// Destructured access

Best practice: Use the by delegate syntax (var count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }) for the most concise code. Always wrap mutableStateOf with remember so the state survives recomposition.

Navigation in Jetpack Compose uses three core components:

NavController:
Manages the navigation back stack and the current destination. Created using rememberNavController().

NavHost:
A composable that displays the current navigation destination. It takes a NavController and a start destination.

composable():
Defines each screen/destination inside the NavHost using a route string.

Example:
@Composable
fun AppNavigation() {
    val navController = rememberNavController()
    NavHost(navController = navController, startDestination = "home") {
        composable("home") { HomeScreen(navController) }
        composable("detail/{id}") { backStackEntry ->
            DetailScreen(id = backStackEntry.arguments?.getString("id"))
        }
    }
}

Navigate to a screen:
navController.navigate("detail/42")

Navigate back:
navController.popBackStack()

A Navigation Graph in Jetpack Compose is a map of all the composable screens (destinations) in the app and the possible routes between them. It is defined inside the NavHost composable using composable() blocks.

It gives you a clear overview of all possible destinations in the app and the paths that connect them, making the navigation flow easier to understand and maintain.

Example:
NavHost(navController = navController, startDestination = "login") {
    composable("login") { LoginScreen(navController) }
    composable("home") { HomeScreen(navController) }
    composable("profile/{userId}") { backStackEntry ->
        ProfileScreen(userId = backStackEntry.arguments?.getString("userId"))
    }
}

The graph shows that the app starts at login, can go to home, and from home can navigate to a profile screen with a userId argument.

If you hardcode Dispatchers.IO directly inside a ViewModel or Use Case, unit testing becomes very hard. Unit tests run instantly on your computer, not on a real Android phone. A computer has no Android Main thread, and background threads make tests unpredictable (the test might finish before the background work even starts).

Solution: DispatcherProvider
Create an interface that supplies dispatchers. In the application code use the real implementation, and in tests use a TestDispatcherProvider that returns Unconfined (or a test dispatcher), so all coroutines run immediately on the test thread.

Example:
interface DispatcherProvider {
    val main: CoroutineDispatcher
    val io: CoroutineDispatcher
    val default: CoroutineDispatcher
}

class DefaultDispatcherProvider : DispatcherProvider {
    override val main = Dispatchers.Main
    override val io = Dispatchers.IO
    override val default = Dispatchers.Default
}

class TestDispatcherProvider : DispatcherProvider {
    override val main = Dispatchers.Unconfined
    override val io = Dispatchers.Unconfined
    override val default = Dispatchers.Unconfined
}

Usage in a ViewModel:
class UserViewModel(private val dispatchers: DispatcherProvider) : ViewModel() {
    fun load() = viewModelScope.launch(dispatchers.io) {
        // work here
    }
}

Dagger was created by developers at Square in 2012. Dagger 2 is a compile-time dependency injection framework for Android. It follows the Java Specification Request (JSR) 330 and uses an annotation processor.

Key annotations:

@Module: Used on a class that constructs objects which are eventually provided as dependencies.

@Provides: Used on methods inside a Module class that return the object.

@Inject: Requests Dagger to provide the respective object. Used on constructors, fields (mostly where the constructor is not accessible, like Activities and Fragments) and methods.

@Component: A Module does not provide dependencies directly to the requesting class. A Component interface acts as a bridge between @Module and @Inject.

@Singleton: Only a single instance of the dependency object is created.

@Scope: Used on an interface to create a custom scope. A scope keeps a single instance of a class as long as the scope exists. Example: @ApplicationScope lives as long as the Application, @ActivityScope lives until the Activity is killed.

@Qualifier: Used when multiple objects of the same type with different values are needed. Example: Student("Vamsi") and Student("Krishna"). A Qualifier tells Dagger which instance to provide.

@Binds: Used on abstract methods in a module to bind an interface or abstract class to its implementation.

@BindsInstance: Used for values received at runtime, such as applicationContext, which can then be used to create other objects.
Example:
val db = Room.databaseBuilder(
    applicationContext,
    AppDatabase::class.java, "database-name"
).build()

Use constructor injection when you own the class and can control its constructor. You simply annotate the constructor with @Inject and Dagger creates the object and supplies its dependencies.

Example:
class UserRepository @Inject constructor(
    private val api: UserApi
)

Use field injection when you cannot use constructor injection. This applies to:
- Android framework classes like Activities, Fragments and Services, whose objects are created by the system.
- Third-party library objects like Retrofit or SharedPreferences, which are created using a builder and cannot have @Inject on their constructors. These are provided from a @Module using @Provides.

Example:
class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {
    @Inject lateinit var repository: UserRepository
}

Design patterns are proven solutions that help create high quality Android apps that are easy to maintain and scale.

The Builder pattern is a creational design pattern that lets you create complex objects step by step. It separates the construction of an object from its representation. In Android it is used for objects like Dialogs, Notifications and Retrofit.

Example:
class Person private constructor(val name: String, val age: Int) {

    class Builder {
        private var name: String = ""
        private var age: Int = 0

        fun name(name: String) = apply { this.name = name }
        fun age(age: Int) = apply { this.age = age }
        fun build() = Person(name, age)
    }
}

val person = Person.Builder()
    .name("Abhijeet")
    .age(30)
    .build()

A Singleton class restricts instantiation to a single instance. No matter how many times or from how many places it is called in the application, it always returns the same instance that was created initially.

In Kotlin, use the object keyword (thread-safe by default):
object MySingleton {
    var count: Int = 0
    fun doSomething() {
        println("Doing something...")
    }
}

In Java, use a private constructor with double-checked locking:
public class MySingleton {
    private static volatile MySingleton instance;

    private MySingleton() { }

    public static MySingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (MySingleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new MySingleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}

The volatile keyword and synchronized block make it thread safe.

The Observer pattern is a behavioral design pattern that defines a one-to-many dependency between objects. When one object (the subject) changes state, all its dependents (observers) are notified and updated automatically.

In Android it is used for event-driven UI updates and publish-subscribe communication between components. LiveData, StateFlow and RxJava are built on this idea.

Example:
interface Observer {
    fun update()
}

class DataModel {
    private val observers = mutableListOf<Observer>()

    fun addObserver(observer: Observer) {
        observers.add(observer)
    }

    fun removeObserver(observer: Observer) {
        observers.remove(observer)
    }

    fun updateData() {
        observers.forEach { it.update() }
    }
}

class Display : Observer {
    override fun update() {
        // update UI with new data
    }
}

fun main() {
    val dataModel = DataModel()
    val display = Display()
    dataModel.addObserver(display)
    dataModel.updateData()
}

The Factory pattern is a creational design pattern that provides an interface for creating objects, while letting subclasses or the factory decide which concrete type to create. The caller does not need to know the creation logic.

In Android it is used to centralize and encapsulate object creation, for example ViewModelProvider.Factory.

Example:
interface Shape {
    fun draw()
}

class Circle : Shape {
    override fun draw() = println("Drawing Circle")
}

class Square : Shape {
    override fun draw() = println("Drawing Square")
}

object ShapeFactory {
    fun create(type: String): Shape = when (type) {
        "circle" -> Circle()
        "square" -> Square()
        else -> throw IllegalArgumentException("Unknown shape")
    }
}

val shape = ShapeFactory.create("circle")
shape.draw()
